{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T20:04:26.608112", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Build the threonine carbon skeleton by converging on 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile. Eight pathways arise from combining two routes to glycine nitrile (stepwise methanimine trapping or one-pot Strecker), two routes to acetaldehyde (hydrothermal pyruvate decarboxylation or UV-driven ethanol dehydrogenation), and two endgame hydrolysis regimes (overall nitrile hydrolysis or stepwise hydrolysis via threonine amide). Two additional pathways come from hydroxyacetone isomerization to lactaldehyde followed by Strecker chemistry and the same two hydrolysis regimes. The network is nonenzymatic and therefore targets the threonine constitutional isomer rather than a single stereoisomer; racemic and diastereomeric threonine/allothreonine mixtures are expected.\n\nMOLECULES (14 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CH3N (methyleneimine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_004: HCN (HCN) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C2H4N2 (glycine nitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C2H6O (ethanol) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H6O2 (acetol) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C3H6O2 (lactaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C4H8N2O (threonine nitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H10N2O2 (threonine amide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H9NO3 (threonine) [target]\n  - mol_014: H2O (water) [starting]\n\nREACTIONS (11 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_003 (Condensation of formaldehyde and ammonia to methanimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_005 (Cyanide capture of methanimine to give glycine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_001, mol_002, mol_004 -> mol_005 (One-pot Strecker synthesis of glycine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_006 -> mol_008 (Hydrothermal decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetaldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_007 -> mol_008 (UV-driven dehydrogenation of ethanol to acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_005, mol_008 -> mol_011 (Aldol-type homologation of glycine nitrile with acetaldehyde) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_007: mol_009 -> mol_010 (Mineral-catalyzed isomerization of hydroxyacetone to lactaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_010, mol_002, mol_004 -> mol_011 (Strecker synthesis of threonine nitrile from lactaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_011, mol_014 -> mol_013 (Overall hydrolysis of threonine nitrile to threonine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_011, mol_014 -> mol_012 (Partial hydrolysis of threonine nitrile to threonine amide) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_012, mol_014 -> mol_013 (Hydrolysis of threonine amide to threonine) [Biochemical]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Stepwise glycine nitrile route with hydrothermal acetaldehyde and direct hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P2: Stepwise glycine nitrile route with photochemical acetaldehyde and direct hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P3: One-pot glycine nitrile route with hydrothermal acetaldehyde and direct hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P4: One-pot glycine nitrile route with photochemical acetaldehyde and direct hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P5: Stepwise glycine nitrile route with hydrothermal acetaldehyde via threonine amide (6 steps)\n  - P6: Stepwise glycine nitrile route with photochemical acetaldehyde via threonine amide (6 steps)\n  - P7: One-pot glycine nitrile route with hydrothermal acetaldehyde via threonine amide (5 steps)\n  - P8: One-pot glycine nitrile route with photochemical acetaldehyde via threonine amide (5 steps)\n  - P9: Hydroxyacetone-lactaldehyde Strecker route with direct hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P10: Hydroxyacetone-lactaldehyde Strecker route via threonine amide (4 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 11 reactions | 4 balanced as written | 0 balanceable with coefficients | 7 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 UNBALANCED: CH2O + NH3 -> CH3N | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product\n  - rxn_003 UNBALANCED: CH2O + NH3 + HCN -> C2H4N2 | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_004 UNBALANCED: C3H4O3 -> C2H4O | reactants minus products: C+1, O+2 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as product\n  - rxn_005 UNBALANCED: C2H6O -> C2H4O | reactants minus products: H+2 | balanceable if adding: H2 as product\n  - rxn_008 UNBALANCED: C3H6O2 + NH3 + HCN -> C4H8N2O | reactants minus products: H+2, O+1 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_009 UNBALANCED: C4H8N2O + H2O -> C4H9NO3 | reactants minus products: H+1, N+1, O-1 | balanceable if adding: NH3 as product\n  - rxn_011 UNBALANCED: C4H10N2O2 + H2O -> C4H9NO3 | reactants minus products: H+3, N+1 | balanceable if adding: NH3 as product\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.52,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.64,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.46,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.24\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.48,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.65,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.41,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.5,\n      \"feedback\": \"P1 is connected and conceptually sensible, but it contains multiple conservation errors and one weakly specified C-C bond-forming step. rxn_001 should release H2O, rxn_004 should release CO2, and rxn_009 should be written overall as mol_011 + 2 mol