, 'Using rxn_010 as an amide stage is more chemically coherent than direct nitrile-to-acid hydrolysis. However, rxn_001 still needs H2O, rxn_004 still needs CO2, rxn_011 must release NH3, and rxn_006 remains the least secure transformation and is misassigned to a Biochemical environment.', 'The amide endgame improves coherence, but the acetaldehyde source is still weakly specified. rxn_001 needs H2O, rxn_005 needs H2 or an explicit oxidant and photocatalyst, rxn_011 must release NH3, and rxn_006 remains only moderately plausible without better nonenzymatic justification.', 'Among the glycine nitrile routes, this is the strongest because one-pot Strecker entry and stepwise hydrolysis are both plausible motifs. It still needs correction of rxn_003 to include H2O, rxn_004 to include CO2, and rxn_011 to release NH3, while rxn_006 still needs stronger nonenzymatic support and more realistic environmental labeling.', 'This keeps the more coherent amide endgame, but the photochemical acetaldehyde route still limits confidence. rxn_003 should include H2O, rxn_005 should include H2 or a stated oxidant and photocatalyst, rxn_011 must release NH3, and rxn_006 remains under-justified for a nonenzymatic network.', 'This branch is mechanistically cleaner than the glycine nitrile homologation routes because rxn_008 follows standard Strecker logic once lactaldehyde is available. Confidence is reduced because rxn_008 omits H2O, rxn_009 omits NH3 and effectively needs two waters overall, and the final hydrolysis is placed in a Biochemical environment rather than explicit nonenzymatic hydrolysis conditions.', 'This is the most coherent path in the network: rxn_007 plus rxn_008 builds the correct carbon skeleton by a plausible isomerization followed by standard Strecker chemistry, and rxn_010 acknowledges the usual amide intermediate. Remaining issues are that rxn_008 should include H2O, rxn_011 should release NH3, and rxn_010 and rxn_011 need explicit nonenzymatic hydrolysis conditions instead of the Biochemical label.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Build the threonine carbon skeleton through lactaldehyde, the most direct nonenzymatic Strecker precursor for a beta-hydroxy alpha-amino acid. A connected inorganic-carbon entry is included through hydrothermal CO2 reduction to formaldehyde, followed by surface formose growth to glyceraldehyde, triose degradation to methylglyoxal, hydrothermal reduction to lactaldehyde, Strecker conversion to the aminonitrile, and explicit nonenzymatic hydrolysis in hot surface waters. The 10 pathways arise from five plausible environmental entry points into this shared carbonyl core crossed with two hydrolysis endgames, and the network deliberately avoids the weaker direct glycine-nitrile homologation route. Stereochemistry is not resolved: the prebiotic product set would include racemic threonine and allothreonine, while the target node tracks the threonine member of that mixture.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h1,3,5-6H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methylglyoxal\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h2-3,5H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"