.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde dimerization to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\", \"carbonate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate/carbonate mineral assemblage\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline evaporitic pool with wet-dry concentration and partially UV-shielded microenvironments\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalyzed formose dimerization of two formaldehyde equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall stoichiometry is 2 CH2O -> C2H4O2. This is the initiating C-C bond-forming step of formose-type chemistry. It is concentration-sensitive and challenging at low conversion, but glycolaldehyde is the canonical first higher sugar and borate-bearing alkaline brines can help stabilize higher-carbonyl products against runaway side reactions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose homologation to glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\", \"carbonate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate/carbonate mineral assemblage\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline evaporitic pool or tidal-flat brine under wet-dry concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol homologation within formose chemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldol addition of formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde grows the carbon skeleton to a triose pool. Glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone are both plausible outcomes under these conditions; only the glyceraldehyde branch is propagated here because it can dehydrate cleanly to methylglyoxal, a direct precursor to lactaldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde dehydration to pyruvaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm evaporitic pools or subaerial geothermal fields with intermittent drying; mildly basic to neutral; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enediolization followed by dehydration and rearrangement to an alpha-oxoaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Triose sugars readily enolize and dehydrate under heating, especially in phosphate-rich or mildly basic media, to give methylglyoxal. Explicit H2O release keeps the node balanced and captures a known degradation path of glyceraldehyde without invoking any unpaired oxidant or reductant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of pyruvaldehyde to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic H2-rich hydrothermal flow through Fe-Ni mineral assemblages\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-surface hydrogenation of the ketone carbonyl of methylglyoxal to give the corresponding alpha-hydroxy aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Compared with the Strecker and hydrolysis steps, this reduction is less directly constrained in prebiotic experiments, but it is chemically straightforward: H2-rich Fe/Ni sulfide or alloy surfaces can deliver the needed two-electron reduction of the keto group of methylglyoxal to lactaldehyde. Selectivity is the key bottleneck, and lactaldehyde would likely coexist with more reduced side products.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of threonine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated NH3 and HCN in evaporitic or partially UV-shielded surface brines; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation from lactaldehyde and ammonia followed by cyanide addition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically strongest way to install the amino nitrile functionality on the correct beta-hydroxy carbon skeleton. Alpha-hydroxy aldehydes undergo standard Strecker chemistry to the corresponding alpha-amino nitriles. Explicit water release keeps the step balanced. Because lactaldehyde is already chiral and cyanide addition creates a new stereocenter, the product is expected to be a racemic threonine and allothreonine nitrile mixture rather than a single stereoisomer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of threonine nitrile to threoninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hot surface waters in subaerial geothermal fields with repeated heating and cooling; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic hydration of the nitrile to 